{{--
  Judge case history — deliberately an empty state, not a table.

  ## What was here

  Five hardcoded cases, rendered on the page of every real, named federal
  judge in the directory:

      CR-2024-001  State v. Johnson        Criminal  Active
      CV-2024-045  Smith v. Corporation    Civil     Pending
      FM-2024-023  In re: Williams Family  Family    Active
      PR-2024-012  Estate of Davis         Probate   Closed
      CR-2024-078  State v. Anderson       Criminal  Active

  plus "Total cases: 1,248 | Active cases: 12", "Showing 1 to 10 of 97
  results", a nine-page paginator, and Filter / Sort buttons that did nothing.
  None of it came from anywhere. Every judge's page showed the same five
  invented dockets with the same invented statistics.

  That is not placeholder text. It reads as a judicial record — case numbers,
  party names, filing dates, case status — attributed to an identifiable
  person, on a site whose purpose is public criticism of that person. Lorem
  ipsum is obviously fake; "State v. Johnson, filed Jan 15 2024, Active" is
  not, and a visitor has no way to tell it apart from a real docket.

  ## Why there is no table yet

  Nothing in this application has case data. The `judges` and `court_houses`
  tables come from CourtListener's bulk export (see
  scripts/ingest-courtlistener.ts) and carry no dockets.

  Real data is reachable, but not cheaply: CourtListener publishes a
  `dockets` bulk table with an `assigned_to` link to the judge — it is about
  5 GB compressed, so it needs a selective import rather than the
  parse-the-whole-file approach the other tables use. The REST API's
  /api/rest/v4/dockets/ is the alternative and needs a token plus a quota
  that will not paginate 2,740 judges' dockets.

  Until one of those lands, an empty state is the honest render. It is also
  the one that does not have to be unwound: whoever wires dockets up replaces
  this file, rather than hunting for which numbers on the page were real.

  No `<script client>` on purpose — there is nothing dynamic here, and an
  empty scope with no bindings is harmless, whereas a scope registered with
  no state/derived/effect call silently kills any binding added later.
--}}

<div class="lg:col-span-2 lg:row-end-2 lg:row-span-2 -mx-4 px-4 py-6 sm:mx-0 sm:pb-10 sm:px-6 xl:pb-16 xl:pt-12 xl:px-12 bg-white ring-1 ring-gray-900/5 sm:rounded-lg shadow-sm">
  <h2 class="font-semibold text-2xl text-gray-900">Case history</h2>

  <div class="mt-8 px-6 py-12 text-center bg-gray-50 border border-dashed border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
    {{--
      No <Icon> here. `folder-open` IS present in the installed hugeicons
      collection, and it still rendered as nothing — verified in the built
      artifact, where the <h3> follows the wrapper div with no <svg> between
      them. 17 of the 23 <Icon> names used across this app are not in the
      installed collection at all (`search`, `chevron-down`, `check`, …), so
      the icon pipeline has a problem wider than one name and worth its own
      pass. Shipping an element that reliably renders nothing is just dead
      markup, so this empty state is type-only.
    --}}
    <h3 class="font-semibold text-base text-gray-900">No case history available</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 mx-auto max-w-md text-gray-600 text-sm">
      Bench Review does not currently publish docket records for this judge. We would
      rather show nothing than show case numbers we cannot stand behind.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 mx-auto max-w-md text-gray-500 text-xs">
      Federal dockets are public record. If you are looking for a specific case,
      <a href="https://www.courtlistener.com/?type=r" class="font-medium text-blue-600 underline hover:text-blue-700" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">CourtListener</a>
      and
      <a href="https://pacer.uscourts.gov" class="font-medium text-blue-600 underline hover:text-blue-700" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">PACER</a>
      both carry them.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="mt-6 text-gray-500 text-xs">
    Reviews on this site describe first-hand courtroom experience. They are not
    linked to docket records, and nothing here should be read as a case outcome.
  </p>
</div>
